    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 062242\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n242 PM PST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Breezy northerly winds are expected today and Saturday, with\n   the strongest winds with elevated wave heights in the Colorado \n   River Valley. \n\n* Quiet weather with near to above normal temperatures expected \n  through at least next Thursday \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Next Friday.\n\nAn extended stretch of relatively quiet and warm weather is expected \nfor the Lower Great Basin and Mojave Desert through at least next \nThursday. The only \"active\" weather to mention are breezy conditions \ntoday and tomorrow, specifically for the Colorado River Valley where \nLake Wind Advisories are in effect. \n\nA sharp upper level trough shifts southeast today placing the region \nin an area of increasing northerly flow, leading to dry conditions \nand breezy north winds today and tomorrow. The surface pressure \ngradient is not overly impressive and widespread gusts 20-30 mph \ngusts with stronger gusts due to terrain enhancement in the Colorado \nRiver Valley are expected. While Laughlin/Bullhead City may briefly \nsee 45 MPH winds, northerly gusts in the Colorado River should \nremain around 30-35 MPH. Impacts to boating due to winds and \nbuilding waves are expected on Lake Mohave and Lake Havasu and a \nLake Wind Advisory is in effect through Saturday afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of the weekend and into next week, a building ridge and \na Polar jet retreating northward will keep the storm track far away \nfrom the region. Guidance continues to show above normal upper level \nheights with this ridge as well as surface to 850mb temperature \nanomalies well above normal for this time of year. This translates \nto high temperatures in the 70s to low 80s and lows in the 50s \ngenerally for desert valleys. Cooler conditions expected for higher \nelevations but still above normal. A weak wave of energy passes \nthrough the ridge early next week; however sensible impacts to \nsurface weather conditions will be minimal as a warm and dry airmass \nremains in place. Expect the ridge to re-strengthen over the Desert \nSouthwest mid next week ahead of the next potential storm system. \nForecast confidence is fairly high through the period with strong \nagreement among ensemble and deterministic guidance. \n\nForecast confidence falls going into the following weekend as a \nstrong trough approaches the west coast. Since this system is more \nthan a week away, expect model disagreement and run to run \ninconsistencies this far out. While confidence is higher in an \noverall pattern change next weekend into the end of the month \nfeaturing troughing and possible chances for precipitation, \nconfidence is lower on the exact details and evolution of that \npattern. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Elevated \nnortherly winds gusting between 20 and 25 knots will continue into \nthe early evening. Sustained wind speeds decrease to less than 10\nknots and direction shifts to the northwest overnight. Lighter \nwinds can be expected tomorrow. VFR conditions continue through \nthe TAF period. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty northerly winds \nshould start to weaken in the evening. In the Colorado River Valley, \npeak gusts will be in the 25 to 35 knot range, decrease slightly \novernight, then strengthen again tomorrow. In the Las Vegas Valley, \npeak gusts will be between 20 and 25 knots, with lighter winds \nexpected overnight and tomorrow. VFR conditions will persist through \nthe TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
